winhttp: don't leave a stale Content-Length behind when decompressing.

winhttp stores response headers twice -- a parsed array (what WinHttpQueryHeaders
answers attribute queries from) and a flat text blob (what WINHTTP_QUERY_RAW_HEADERS
and _RAW_HEADERS_CRLF return verbatim). When WINHTTP_OPTION_DECOMPRESSION is on and
the response is gzip/deflate, init_gzip_stream() deletes Content-Length because the
number describes the COMPRESSED body while WinHttpReadData() now yields the
DECOMPRESSED one. Upstream's remove_header() only edits the parsed array, and only
its first match. Two ways out of that survive, both measured on stock 11.10-79 with
a localhost harness serving 403 compressed / 8193 decompressed bytes:

  1. RAW_HEADERS_CRLF still contains "Content-Length: 403" while the attribute query
     already says HEADER_NOT_FOUND and ReadData delivers 8193 -- the same header of
     the same response reported two different ways at the same instant.
  2. If the response carries Content-Length TWICE (a proxy re-adding it, say), the
     second copy survives remove_header() entirely and the plain attribute query
     returns 403 against an 8193-byte body:
       VERDICT UNSAFE reported=403 actual=8193 overrun=7790

A caller sizing a buffer from either one under-allocates by the compression ratio.
On a build tester's machine AdobeGrowthSDK.dll did exactly that -- 1512 allocated,
8193 copied, a 6681-byte overrun into a following FREE block whose base_offset was
overwritten with 0x3039, the ASCII "90" out of the JSON being copied
("...cpuThreshold":90,"__stage__growth.contentC...). RtlAllocateHeap later derived a
subheap pointer from that field and hung the process into a node.exe crash loop. The
sequence was captured by the 11.10-79 heap guard.

Remove Content-Length from the raw blob as well, and drop every copy from the parsed
array rather than just the first. request->content_length -- the INTERNAL read-loop
bound, which upstream already parks at ~0ull here -- is untouched, as is
Content-Encoding in both stores.

Gate NEUTRON_WINHTTP_GZIP_HEADERS: 0 restores the pre-patch behaviour exactly,
1 (default) applies the fix.

--- a/dlls/winhttp/request.c
+++ b/dlls/winhttp/request.c
@@ -2731,6 +2731,107 @@
     return ERROR_WINHTTP_LOGIN_FAILURE;
 }
 
+/* =============================================================================================
+ * NEUTRON: winhttp decompression leaves a stale Content-Length in the RAW header blob.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS WRONG.  winhttp keeps the response headers TWICE: a parsed array (request->headers[],
+ * what WinHttpQueryHeaders() answers attribute queries from) and a flat text blob
+ * (request->raw_headers, what WINHTTP_QUERY_RAW_HEADERS / _RAW_HEADERS_CRLF hand back verbatim).
+ * read_reply() builds both.  When WINHTTP_OPTION_DECOMPRESSION is on and the response carries
+ * Content-Encoding: gzip/deflate, init_gzip_stream() installs a decompressing data_stream and
+ * -- correctly -- deletes Content-Length, because the number describes the COMPRESSED body while
+ * WinHttpReadData() now yields the DECOMPRESSED one.  But remove_header() -> delete_header()
+ * only edits the parsed ARRAY.  Nothing touches raw_headers.  So the two query paths disagree
+ * about the same header of the same response at the same instant:
+ *
+ *     WinHttpQueryHeaders( WINHTTP_QUERY_CONTENT_LENGTH )   -> ERROR_WINHTTP_HEADER_NOT_FOUND
+ *     WinHttpQueryHeaders( WINHTTP_QUERY_RAW_HEADERS_CRLF ) -> "... Content-Length: 403 ..."
+ *     WinHttpReadData()                                     -> 8193 bytes
+ *
+ * (Those three lines are literal output of the localhost harness on unpatched 11.10-79.)
+ *
+ * WHY IT MATTERS.  A caller that sizes a buffer by scanning the raw blob -- an ordinary thing to
+ * do, and the only way to read a header winhttp has no attribute constant for -- allocates the
+ * compressed size and then copies in the decompressed body.  On a build tester's machine Adobe's
+ * AdobeGrowthSDK.dll did exactly that: 1512 bytes allocated, 8193 written, a 6681-byte heap
+ * overrun that walked into a following FREE block and overwrote its base_offset with 0x3039 --
+ * the ASCII "90" out of the JSON text  "...cpuThreshold":90,"__stage__growth.contentC...  that
+ * was being copied.  RtlAllocateHeap later derived a subheap pointer from that field and hung
+ * the process; the node.exe helper respawned into a crash loop.  The whole sequence was captured
+ * by the 11.10-79 heap guard (NEUTRON_HEAP_BASE_GUARD), which is how the writer was identified.
+ *
+ * WHAT THIS DOES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.
+ *  - It removes Content-Length from raw_headers as well, so the blob agrees with the array.
+ *    That is the whole fix: make the two reports of one header consistent.
+ *  - It also removes any DUPLICATE Content-Length from the parsed array.  Upstream's
+ *    remove_header() drops only index 0, so a second copy of a header we have just decided is
+ *    a lie would survive and be reported as truth.
+ *  - It does NOT touch request->content_length.  That is the INTERNAL read-loop bound, and
+ *    upstream already parks it at ~0ull here so end_of_data_stream()/the min() clamps defer to
+ *    the gzip stream's own end-of-data.  Reported metadata and the read loop are separate
+ *    concerns and only the reported side is changed.
+ *  - It does NOT touch Content-Encoding, in either store.  Leaving it is upstream's deliberate
+ *    choice, it cannot cause an under-allocation, and stripping it would change what every
+ *    caller sees about how a response was transferred -- winhttp is on Adobe's licensing/IMS
+ *    sign-in path, so the narrower change is the right one.  A stale Content-Encoding at worst
+ *    makes a caller try to inflate an already-inflated body and fail loudly; a stale
+ *    Content-Length makes it corrupt the heap silently.
+ *  - It allocates nothing and can fail nothing: the removal is an in-place memmove that only
+ *    ever SHRINKS a NUL-terminated buffer that is already owned by the request.
+ *
+ * KILL SWITCH.  NEUTRON_WINHTTP_GZIP_HEADERS=0 restores the pre-patch behaviour exactly, stale
+ * blob included.  1 (default) applies the fix.  Named to match NEUTRON_MAXKEEP_FIX /
+ * NEUTRON_HEAP_BASE_GUARD.
+ * ============================================================================================= */
+
+static BOOL neutron_winhttp_gzip_headers_fix(void)
+{
+    static int enabled = -1;
+
+    if (enabled == -1)
+    {
+        WCHAR buf[8];
+        DWORD len = GetEnvironmentVariableW( L"NEUTRON_WINHTTP_GZIP_HEADERS", buf, ARRAY_SIZE(buf) );
+        enabled = (len && len < ARRAY_SIZE(buf) && buf[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1;
+        /* One-shot at ERR so a capture can tell "gate off" from "patch absent". */
+        ERR( "neutron-winhttp: gate NEUTRON_WINHTTP_GZIP_HEADERS resolved to %d\n", enabled );
+    }
+    return enabled == 1;
+}
+
+/* Delete every "<field>: ..." line from the raw header blob.  The blob is a NUL-terminated
+ * WCHAR string of CRLF-separated lines whose first line is the status line (never a header,
+ * so it is skipped) and which ends with an empty line.  Removal is a memmove of the tail over
+ * the matched line, so the buffer only shrinks and stays NUL-terminated throughout. */
+static void neutron_raw_headers_remove( struct request *request, const WCHAR *field )
+{
+    SIZE_T fieldlen = wcslen( field );
+    WCHAR *p = request->raw_headers, *eol;
+
+    if (!p) return;
+    if (!(p = wcsstr( p, L"\r\n" ))) return;   /* skip the status line */
+    p += 2;
+
+    while (*p && (eol = wcsstr( p, L"\r\n" )) && eol != p)
+    {
+        WCHAR *sep = p;
+
+        if (!wcsnicmp( p, field, fieldlen ))
+        {
+            sep = p + fieldlen;
+            while (*sep == ' ' || *sep == '\t') sep++;
+        }
+        if (sep != p && *sep == ':')
+        {
+            WCHAR *rest = eol + 2;
+            TRACE( "neutron: dropping stale %s from the raw headers\n", debugstr_w(field) );
+            memmove( p, rest, (wcslen( rest ) + 1) * sizeof(WCHAR) );
+            continue;                          /* re-examine at the same position */
+        }
+        p = eol + 2;
+    }
+}
+
 static DWORD init_gzip_stream( struct request *request, BOOL is_gzip )
 {
     struct gzip_stream *gzip_stream;
@@ -2760,7 +2861,13 @@
     gzip_stream->parent = request->data_stream;
     request->data_stream = &gzip_stream->data_stream;
 
-    remove_header( request, L"Content-Length", FALSE );
+    if (neutron_winhttp_gzip_headers_fix())
+    {
+        while (get_header_index( request, L"Content-Length", 0, FALSE ) != -1)
+            remove_header( request, L"Content-Length", FALSE );
+        neutron_raw_headers_remove( request, L"Content-Length" );
+    }
+    else remove_header( request, L"Content-Length", FALSE );
     request->content_length = ~0ull;
     return ERROR_SUCCESS;
 }
